          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had reflux for years and now food sometimes sticks going down. How worrying is that, and what does guidance describe about when to investigate?",
          "answer": "Food sticking when you swallow is taken seriously in the guidance. It is not an emergency by the tool's check, but it should not be left to wait.\n\nThe gastroenterology guideline (*GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it*) lists difficulty swallowing as an alarm feature. When one is present, it recommends prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the upper gut) rather than simply trying reflux treatment and waiting to see. The general GERD reference (*Gastroesophageal reflux disease*) explains why: long-standing acid exposure can cause narrowing of the food pipe (stricture) and difficulty swallowing. It can also change the lining of the gullet (Barrett's oesophagus), which carries a small increased risk of cancer. The reference notes that this risk is why long-standing reflux with alarm features is investigated rather than assumed to be simple reflux.\n\nThe same guideline lists other alarm features to report to a clinician: unintended weight loss, vomiting blood or black tarry stools, iron-deficiency anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump in the upper abdomen, a family history of upper gut cancer, and new symptoms starting at an older age.\n\nPlease contact your doctor soon about the swallowing change rather than waiting. Seek urgent care straight away if you have trouble swallowing saliva, vomit blood, pass black stools, or have chest pain or breathing difficulty.\n\nThe sources did not cover how long to wait, which tests would be used for you, or anything about your own risk. Those are questions for your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
